1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,520
 All right, everybody, you've made it.

2
00:00:01,520 --> 00:00:05,360
 With the exception of the advanced stuff that's coming right at the end of the course,

3
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:09,600
 we've reached the last section of kind of core material that we're going to cover,

4
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:11,200
 the last two weeks of content.

5
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:12,720
 Now, there are two of them.

6
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:15,040
 This week, we're going to meet engineering composites,

7
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:20,480
 amalgams of two or three or more engineering materials that come together to give you some

8
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:24,240
 effective properties that might be better than the sum of the parts.

9
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:28,320
 Engineering composites are normally constructed to leverage the best aspects of the constituents

10
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:33,280
 that make them up and to minimize the effects of the weaknesses of these constituents.

11
00:00:33,280 --> 00:00:37,920
 And as you can imagine, the properties and behaviors of these constituents are fundamental

12
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:42,320
 to the effective properties and behaviors of the composites that are made from them.

13
00:00:42,320 --> 00:00:46,400
 It's one of the reasons why this had to come right at the end after all of our studies

14
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:50,000
 and why you needed to have such a good understanding of all of these different

15
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,680
 constituent families of materials to be able to talk about composites.

16
00:00:53,680 --> 00:00:57,760
 From the outset and first of all, I might recognize that the word composite is quite broad

17
00:00:57,760 --> 00:01:00,240
 and has different meanings depending on who you speak to.

18
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:04,320
 And while the word composite might conjure up images in your mind of complicated,

19
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:08,480
 organic geometries made from carbon fiber, it does have a broader definition.

20
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:11,520
 Most of the engineering materials that we've already met this semester,

21
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:16,160
 whether or not they appear to be pure, actually composites of one kind or another.

22
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:22,560
 For example, even pure mild steel, my favorite material is a composite of pure iron and cementite,

23
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:24,000
 a metal and a ceramic.

24
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,720
 And aluminium alloys are the same, a composite of a pure aluminium core with an

25
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:32,800
 alumina oxide layer, a ceramic oxide layer on the outside.

26
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:36,720
 In general, whether you classify a material as a composite depends a little bit on context

27
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:38,560
 and can be a little bit subjective.

28
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:43,200
 Some people, for example, might classify any mixture of pure elements as a composite or

29
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:47,280
 any mixture of unlike geometries of a single element as a composite.

30
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:50,880
 In this course, we're going to take a slightly more restrictive definition,

31
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:56,800
 classifying a composite as a mixture of at least two distinct phases of materials.

32
00:01:56,800 --> 00:01:59,520
 This definition captures the obvious examples of composites,

33
00:01:59,520 --> 00:02:03,280
 the ones you're probably familiar with, fiber reinforced polymers, for example.

34
00:02:03,280 --> 00:02:07,200
 But also captures some of the more nebulous ones, the mild steels, for example,

35
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:10,240
 that have one material entombed within another.

36
00:02:10,240 --> 00:02:14,560
 Importantly, our definition of a composite is going to exclude materials which are a

37
00:02:14,560 --> 00:02:18,560
 mixture of two or more elements, but this happens in an interesting way.

38
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:22,480
 Like in an alloy where one material might be substitutionally or interstitually

39
00:02:22,480 --> 00:02:28,080
 alloyed into another, but becomes part of the same matrix and isn't distinct from the other.

40
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:31,520
 This week, as has been the trend in this course, we're going to focus more on the

41
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:35,840
 material properties of composites and how these arise as a function of their constituents

42
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:36,720
 and their geometry.

43
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:40,640
 And we'll meet a few characteristic subfamilies of composites as well.

44
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:44,080
 Next week, we're going to focus more on manufacturing of engineering composites

45
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:47,520
 and the different techniques that are used to form and shape these.

46
00:02:47,520 --> 00:02:50,480
 So sit back, relax and enjoy the videos for this week.

47
00:02:50,480 --> 00:02:54,480
 The last of the concept introduction videos we're going to have in this course.
